  {
   "id": "q001",
   "question": "Do creatures phasing back in trigger enter the battlefield abilities?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2404,
    "completion_tokens": 310,
    "total_tokens": 2714,
    "cost": 0.000267552,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000267552,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000211752,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 5.58e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, creatures phasing back in do not trigger enter-the-battlefield abilities. The phasing event does not cause a permanent to change zones; it only changes the permanent's status from 'phased out' to 'phased in' (rule [702.26c]). Because the permanent never leaves or enters the battlefield, zone-change triggers such as 'enters the battlefield' abilities do not trigger (rule [702.26d]). Additionally, rule [603.6a] specifies that enters-the-battlefield triggers occur when a permanent enters the battlefield, which phasing does not do. Rule [702.26j] further clarifies that abilities triggering when a permanent becomes attached or unattached also do not trigger on phasing, reinforcing that phasing is not a zone-change event.",
   "citations": [
    "702.26c",
    "702.26d",
    "603.6a",
    "702.26j"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q002",
   "question": "how does trample work with deathtouch?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2119,
    "completion_tokens": 407,
    "total_tokens": 2526,
    "cost": 0.000259362,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000259362,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000186102,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 7.326e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "When a creature with both trample and deathtouch assigns combat damage, deathtouch modifies what counts as “lethal damage” for the purpose of trample’s damage assignment. Specifically, any nonzero amount of combat damage from a source with deathtouch is considered lethal damage to a creature [702.2c]. This means that when assigning damage to blocking creatures, the attacking creature’s controller need only assign 1 damage to each blocker (regardless of that blocker’s toughness) to satisfy the lethal-damage requirement, and then all remaining damage can be assigned to the defending player, planeswalker, or battle as normal under the trample rules [702.19b]. For example, a 5/5 creature with trample and deathtouch blocked by a 3/3 can assign 1 damage to the blocker (lethal due to deathtouch) and 4 damage to the defending player. The same principle applies to trample over planeswalkers [702.19c] and to excess-damage effects [120.4a].",
   "citations": [
    "702.2c",
    "702.19b",
    "702.19c",
    "120.4a",
    "Lethal Damage",
    "Excess Damage"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q003",
   "question": "is storm a triggered ability that can be countered?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2270,
    "completion_tokens": 218,
    "total_tokens": 2488,
    "cost": 0.000238932,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000238932,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000199692,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 3.924e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Storm is a triggered ability, and as such it is not a spell. According to rule [113.9], activated and triggered abilities on the stack are not spells and cannot be countered by anything that counters only spells. They can only be countered by effects that specifically counter abilities. Therefore, storm itself cannot be countered by typical counterspells (which counter spells), but it could be countered by an effect that explicitly says it counters a triggered ability.",
   "citations": [
    "702.40a",
    "113.9"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q004",
   "question": "can i use cards with hybrid mana symbols in a commander deck that only has one of those colors?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2195,
    "completion_tokens": 344,
    "total_tokens": 2539,
    "cost": 0.000254862,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000254862,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000192942,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 6.192e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you cannot use a card with a hybrid mana symbol in a Commander deck if the card’s color identity includes a color that is not in your commander’s color identity. Under [903.5c], a card can be included in a Commander deck only if every color in its color identity is also found in the color identity of the deck’s commander. A hybrid mana symbol is all of its component colors ([107.4e]), so a card with a hybrid symbol like {G/W} has a color identity that includes both green and white. If your commander’s color identity is only red and green, that card’s white component is not in the commander’s color identity, so the card is illegal. This applies regardless of which half you intend to pay; the color identity is determined by the symbol itself, not by how you pay it. The same principle applies to cards brought from outside the game via [903.11a].",
   "citations": [
    "903.5c",
    "107.4e",
    "903.11a"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q005",
   "question": "can I respond to the untap step?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1676,
    "completion_tokens": 255,
    "total_tokens": 1931,
    "cost": 0.000192132,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000192132,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000146232,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 4.59e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you cannot respond to the untap step. According to rule [502.4], no player receives priority during the untap step, so no spells can be cast and no abilities can be activated. The untap step is a step in which no players receive priority, as stated in [500.3]. Any triggered abilities that trigger during this step are held until the next time a player would receive priority, which is usually during the upkeep step ([502.4], [503.1a]). Therefore, you cannot take any actions (such as casting spells or activating abilities) during the untap step itself.",
   "citations": [
    "[502.4]",
    "[500.3]",
    "[503.1a]"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q006",
   "question": "are creatures still considered attacking creatures during the end of combat step?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2331,
    "completion_tokens": 319,
    "total_tokens": 2650,
    "cost": 0.000262602,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000262602,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000205182,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 5.742e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Yes, creatures are still considered attacking creatures during the end of combat step. According to rule [508.1k], a creature remains an attacking creature until it is removed from combat or the combat phase ends, whichever comes first. The end of combat step is part of the combat phase (see rule [511.3]), so creatures that were declared as attackers or put onto the battlefield attacking earlier in the phase remain attacking creatures throughout this step. They are only removed from combat at the very end of the combat phase, after the end of combat step ends (rule [511.3] and [506.4a]). Effects that last 'until end of combat' expire at the end of the combat phase, not during the end of combat step (rule [500.5a] and [511.2]).",
   "citations": [
    "508.1k",
    "511.3",
    "506.4a",
    "500.5a",
    "511.2"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q007",
   "question": "Do you cast lands?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1958,
    "completion_tokens": 316,
    "total_tokens": 2274,
    "cost": 0.000228492,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000228492,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000171612,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 5.688e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you do not cast lands. Lands are never cast as spells. Playing a land is a special action that does not use the stack, as defined by [305.1], [505.6b], and [701.18a]. A land card has no mana cost ([202.1b]), and if an object is both a land and another card type, it can only be played as a land, not cast as a spell ([305.9], [300.2a]). The term \"play\" for a card means either playing it as a land or casting it as a spell, whichever is appropriate ([701.18b], [Play]). Since lands are played, not cast, they cannot be countered or responded to with instants or activated abilities ([305.1], [505.6b]).",
   "citations": [
    "305.1",
    "505.6b",
    "701.18a",
    "202.1b",
    "305.9",
    "300.2a",
    "701.18b",
    "Play"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q008",
   "question": "can I sacrifice an evoked creature before that creature's evoke triggered ability resolves?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2363,
    "completion_tokens": 427,
    "total_tokens": 2790,
    "cost": 0.000284922,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000284922,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000208062,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 7.686e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you cannot sacrifice an evoked creature before its own evoke triggered ability resolves. The evoke ability (rule 702.74a) consists of two parts: an alternative cost to cast the spell, and a triggered ability that says \"When this permanent enters, if its evoke cost was paid, its controller sacrifices it.\" This triggered ability goes on the stack the next time a player would receive priority after the creature enters the battlefield (rule 603.3d). You, as the controller, do not receive priority to activate any abilities (including sacrificing the creature) before that triggered ability is put onto the stack and resolves. The sacrifice from evoke is mandatory and happens as part of the triggered ability's resolution; you cannot preempt it by sacrificing the creature yourself using another ability or cost, because you never have priority between the creature entering and the evoke trigger being put on the stack. Additionally, rule 118.10 confirms that paying a cost for one spell or ability does not satisfy another cost—so sacrificing the creature for another reason would not fulfill the evoke trigger's sacrifice requirement; the evoke trigger would still resolve and attempt to sacrifice it, but if the creature is already gone, the sacrifice instruction does nothing (the creature is already in the graveyard). However, the question is about timing: you cannot sacrifice it *before* the evoke trigger resolves because you lack priority to do so.",
   "citations": [
    "702.74a",
    "603.3d",
    "118.10"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q009",
   "question": "if a card loses all abilities, does that include keyword abilities?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2042,
    "completion_tokens": 349,
    "total_tokens": 2391,
    "cost": 0.000241992,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000241992,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000179172,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 6.282e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Yes. Losing all abilities includes keyword abilities. Rule [113.10b] states that an effect which removes an ability removes all instances of it. Keyword abilities (defined in [702.1]) are abilities, so they are removed by an effect that says an object loses all abilities. This is confirmed by [702.22b], which explicitly states that if an effect causes a permanent to lose banding, it loses all 'bands with other' abilities as well, and by [702.11e], which states that losing hexproof causes loss of all 'hexproof from [quality]' abilities. The layer system in [613.1f] applies ability-removing effects in layer 6, which removes all abilities, including keywords. Note that [113.12] distinguishes between granting an ability and setting a characteristic or stating a quality—'loses all abilities' removes abilities, not characteristics or qualities.",
   "citations": [
    "113.10b",
    "702.1",
    "702.22b",
    "702.11e",
    "613.1f",
    "113.12"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q010",
   "question": "can i draw cards if i'm already at my maximum hand size?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1983,
    "completion_tokens": 323,
    "total_tokens": 2306,
    "cost": 0.000232002,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000232002,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000173862,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 5.814e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Yes, you can draw cards even if you already have a number of cards in your hand equal to your maximum hand size. The maximum hand size only matters during the cleanup step of a turn, when the active player must discard down to that number (see rules [514.1], [703.4n], [402.2]). Drawing cards is a separate action that is not restricted by your current hand size; you simply draw the card(s) and then, if it is the cleanup step, you may need to discard excess cards afterward. There is no rule that prevents drawing while at maximum hand size. For example, if your maximum hand size is seven and you already have seven cards in hand, you can still draw a card—you will then have eight cards, and you will discard one during the cleanup step if it is that step. This applies in all game formats, including multiplayer and Commander, as the same cleanup step rules apply to all players.",
   "citations": [
    "514.1",
    "703.4n",
    "402.2"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
