     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000208332,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000159372,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 4.896e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you cannot respond to a mana ability. A mana ability does not use the stack [605.3b], so it cannot be targeted, countered, or otherwise responded to [605.3b]. It resolves immediately after it is activated [605.3b]. This applies to both activated mana abilities and triggered mana abilities [605.4a]. The only actions that can be taken while a mana ability is resolving are activating other mana abilities (if needed to pay costs) or taking special actions (e.g., turning a face-down permanent face up) [702.61b], but no player can respond to the mana ability itself with spells or other non-mana abilities.",
   "citations": [
    "605.3b",
    "605.4a",
    "702.61b"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q016",
   "question": "can I respond to a cost being paid?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2267,
    "completion_tokens": 419,
    "total_tokens": 2686,
    "cost": 0.000205722,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 1024,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000205722,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000130302,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 7.542e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you cannot respond to a cost being paid. Paying a cost is part of the process of casting a spell or activating an ability, and it occurs while that spell or ability is being put onto the stack, before any player receives priority. Specifically, during the casting of a spell, after the total cost is determined, the player pays all costs in any order (rule 601.2h). This payment happens as part of the casting process, and no player has priority to respond during that process. Similarly, when declaring attackers or blockers, costs (such as those from abilities that require paying mana) are paid during the declaration step, and no player receives priority until after the declaration is complete (rules 508.1i, 509.1e). Once a cost is paid, the game state has changed, but the opportunity to respond comes only after the spell or ability is fully on the stack and a player would receive priority. Therefore, you cannot 'respond' to the payment itself; you can only respond to the spell or ability after it has been cast or activated and is on the stack, or to the triggered abilities that may result from the payment (e.g., a creature being sacrificed).",
   "citations": [
    "601.2g",
    "601.2h",
    "508.1i",
    "509.1e",
    "118.2",
    "118.3",
    "118.10",
    "602.4",
    "604.5",
    "608.2g"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q017",
   "question": "can you explain layers?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2667,
    "completion_tokens": 735,
    "total_tokens": 3402,
    "cost": 0.000298602,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 1024,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000298602,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000166302,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 0.0001323
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Layers are a system used by the game to determine the order in which continuous effects are applied to an object's characteristics. The process starts with the object's actual characteristics (printed on a card, or defined by the effect that created a token or copy) [613.1]. Then, all applicable continuous effects are applied in a series of layers, in the following order [613.1a–613.1f]:\n\n- **Layer 1**: Rules and effects that modify copiable values (including copy effects) [613.1a]. Within this layer, sublayers apply in order: Layer 1a applies copiable effects (e.g., copy effects and “as … enters” abilities that set power/toughness) [613.2a]. After layer 1, the object’s characteristics become its copiable values [613.2c].\n- **Layer 2**: Control-changing effects.\n- **Layer 3**: Text-changing effects [613.1c].\n- **Layer 4**: Type-changing effects (card type, subtype, supertype) [613.1d].\n- **Layer 5**: Color-changing effects [613.1e].\n- **Layer 6**: Ability-adding effects, keyword counters, ability-removing effects, and effects that say an object can’t have an ability [613.1f].\n- **Layer 7**: Power- and toughness-changing effects. This layer has sublayers applied in order: Layer 7a (characteristic-defining abilities that set power/toughness), Layer 7b (effects that set power and/or toughness to a specific value), and Layer 7c (effects that modify power and/or toughness without setting them) [613.4a].\n\nWithin each layer or sublayer, effects are applied in timestamp order, unless a dependency exists (where one effect depends on another, overriding timestamp order) [613.3][613.8][613.8a]. If an effect applies in multiple layers or sublayers, each part applies in its appropriate layer/sublayer [613.6]. For example, an effect that says “This creature gets +1/+1 and becomes the color of your choice” applies the color change in layer 5 and the +1/+1 in layer 7c [613.6]. The game automatically and continuously applies these layers to determine the final characteristics of each object [613.5].",
   "citations": [
    "613.1",
    "613.1a",
    "613.1c",
    "613.1d",
    "613.1e",
    "613.1f",
    "613.2a",
    "613.2c",
    "613.3",
    "613.4a",
    "613.5",
    "613.6",
    "613.8",
    "613.8a"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q018",
   "question": "how does the legend rule work?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1811,
    "completion_tokens": 495,
    "total_tokens": 2306,
    "cost": 0.000247482,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000247482,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000158382,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 8.91e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "The legend rule is a state-based action that applies when a player controls two or more legendary permanents with the same name. When this condition is checked (before a player would receive priority), that player chooses one of those permanents to keep, and the rest are put into their owners’ graveyards. This is defined in rule [704.5j] and the supertype 'legendary' is described in [205.4d]. The rule applies to any permanent with the legendary supertype, including planeswalkers that have received errata to be legendary (see [306.4]). It does not apply to non-legendary permanents or to permanents with different names, even if they share a creature type or other characteristic. The legend rule is separate from the Role rule ([303.7a], [704.5y]) and the world rule ([704.5k], [205.4f]), which are different state-based actions for different supertypes or subtypes. In multiplayer or Commander games, the legend rule works the same way: each player individually checks their own controlled legendary permanents with the same name and must put all but one into their owner’s graveyard. There is no special exception for commanders—if a player controls two legendary permanents with the same name (e.g., two copies of the same legendary creature, even if one is a commander), the legend rule applies normally. The rule does not involve the stack and cannot be responded to (see [117.2d]).",
   "citations": [
    "704.5j",
    "205.4d",
    "306.4",
    "303.7a",
    "704.5y",
    "704.5k",
    "205.4f",
    "117.2d"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q019",
   "question": "when does my creature die after its been marked with a lethal amount of damage?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2001,
    "completion_tokens": 468,
    "total_tokens": 2469,
    "cost": 0.000135306,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 1792,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000135306,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 5.1066e-05,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 8.424e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Your creature dies as a state-based action the next time state-based actions are checked, which occurs just before a player would receive priority. This happens after the spell or ability that dealt the lethal damage finishes resolving. For example, if Lightning Bolt deals 3 damage to a 2/2 creature, the creature is not destroyed during the resolution of Lightning Bolt; instead, after Lightning Bolt has fully resolved and before any player gets priority, state-based actions are checked. At that point, the game sees the creature has lethal damage marked on it (toughness 2, damage 3) and destroys it as a state-based action per rule 704.5g. This destruction does not use the stack (rule 405.6f). If an effect ends the turn or combat phase (e.g., Mandate of Peace), state-based actions are checked as part of that process as well (rules 724.1c, 724.2c). Damage marked on a creature is removed during the cleanup step (rule 514.2), but if the creature has lethal damage before then, it will be destroyed by state-based actions first. In multiplayer or Commander, the same timing applies: state-based actions are checked before any player gets priority, regardless of the number of players.",
   "citations": [
    "120.5",
    "120.6",
    "302.7",
    "704.3",
    "704.5g",
    "405.6f",
    "117.2d",
    "117.5",
    "724.1c",
    "724.2c",
    "514.2",
    "514.3a",
    "703.4p"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
