  {
   "id": "q022",
   "question": "when are state-based actions checked?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1702,
    "completion_tokens": 424,
    "total_tokens": 2126,
    "cost": 0.000224892,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000224892,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000148572,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 7.632e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "State-based actions are checked immediately before a player would receive priority, as part of a repeated process that also handles triggered abilities. Specifically, whenever a player would get priority (see rule 117.5), the game first performs all applicable state-based actions simultaneously as a single event (rule 704.3). If any state-based actions are performed, the check is repeated; otherwise, triggered abilities waiting to be put on the stack are put on the stack, and then the check is repeated again. This loop continues until no state-based actions are performed and no triggered abilities are waiting, at which point the appropriate player gets priority (rule 704.3). State-based actions are also checked during the cleanup step (rule 514.3a) and when an effect ends the turn or the combat phase (rules 724.1c, 724.2c). They are not controlled by any player and do not use the stack (rules 704.1, 704.1a, 405.6f). Turn-based actions, by contrast, are checked at the beginning of steps or phases before state-based actions (rule 703.3).",
   "citations": [
    "704.3",
    "117.5",
    "704.1",
    "704.1a",
    "405.6f",
    "514.3a",
    "724.1c",
    "724.2c",
    "703.3"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q024",
   "question": "when can I concede a game?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1517,
    "completion_tokens": 338,
    "total_tokens": 1855,
    "cost": 0.000192762,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000192762,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000131922,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 6.084e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "You can concede a game at any time. This is a fundamental rule that overrides all other rules and card effects. Specifically, rule [104.3a] states that a player can concede the game at any time, and that player leaves the game immediately and loses. Rule [723.6] reinforces that even if another player controls you, you may still concede at any time. Rule [405.6g] confirms that conceding does not use the stack and happens immediately. Rule [101.1] explicitly states that the ability to concede at any time is the only exception to the rule that card text overrides the basic rules. In Two-Headed Giant, rule [810.8b] adds that if a player concedes, their entire team leaves the game and loses. There is no restriction on when you can concede—it can be during any phase, step, or even in response to a spell or ability, and it cannot be prevented by any game effect.",
   "citations": [
    "104.3a",
    "723.6",
    "405.6g",
    "101.1",
    "810.8b"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q025",
   "question": "how do I know who the active player is?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1228,
    "completion_tokens": 313,
    "total_tokens": 1541,
    "cost": 0.000162252,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000162252,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000105912,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 5.634e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "The active player is defined as the player whose turn it is ([102.1]). In a standard multiplayer game, there is exactly one active player at any given time—the player currently taking their turn. If the game uses the shared team turns option (rule 805), the team whose turn it is is the active team, and the concept of a single active player is replaced by the active team ([805.4a]). In that case, any ability that refers to the 'active player' requires its controller to choose which specific player on the active team the ability refers to when the effect is applied ([805.9]). To determine the active player, simply look at whose turn it is; if it's a team turn, the active player is whichever member of the active team the ability's controller chooses at the time the effect applies.",
   "citations": [
    "[102.1]",
    "[805.4a]",
    "[805.9]"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q026",
   "question": "when someone other than the active player casts a spell, who gets priority first?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 1505,
    "completion_tokens": 302,
    "total_tokens": 1807,
    "cost": 0.000185202,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000185202,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000130842,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 5.436e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "After a spell (other than a mana ability) resolves, the active player receives priority, regardless of who cast the spell. This is stated in rule [117.3b]: 'The active player receives priority after a spell or ability (other than a mana ability) resolves.' The identity of the caster does not change this rule. Additionally, if a player casts a spell while they have priority, rule [117.3c] says that player receives priority afterward, but that applies only to the moment immediately after casting, not after the spell later resolves. The question asks about the moment after a spell resolves, so [117.3b] governs. The active player is the player whose turn it is (see [101.4d] for definition). Therefore, after any spell resolves, the active player gets priority first.",
   "citations": [
    "117.3b",
    "117.3c",
    "101.4d"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q027",
   "question": "when the active player cast a spell, who gets priority first?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
