   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Lore counters are put on Sagas during the precombat main phase of the active player's turn, as a turn-based action that does not use the stack. Specifically, immediately after a player's precombat main phase begins, that player puts a lore counter on each Saga they control that has one or more chapter abilities. This is defined in rules [714.3c], [703.4f], and [505.4]. Additionally, a Saga with read ahead enters the battlefield with a chosen number of lore counters (between one and its final chapter number) as a replacement effect, per [714.3b] and [702.155b]. A Saga without read ahead enters with one lore counter as a replacement effect, per [714.3a]. The turn-based action of adding a lore counter happens each precombat main phase, not just on the turn the Saga enters. If the number of lore counters on a Saga equals or exceeds its final chapter number and no chapter ability is still on the stack, the Saga is sacrificed as a state-based action, per [714.4] and [704.5s]. Note that read ahead prevents chapter abilities from triggering on the turn the Saga entered unless the number of lore counters exactly matches the chapter symbol's number, per [702.155a].",
   "citations": [
    "714.3c",
    "703.4f",
    "505.4",
    "714.3b",
    "702.155b",
    "714.3a",
    "714.4",
    "704.5s",
    "702.155a"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q030",
   "question": "what is a delayed triggered ability?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2204,
    "completion_tokens": 561,
    "total_tokens": 2765,
    "cost": 0.000151884,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 2048,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000151884,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 5.0904e-05,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 0.00010098
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "A delayed triggered ability is a triggered ability created by the effects of resolving spells or abilities, replacement effects, or static abilities that allow a player to take an action. It contains 'when,' 'whenever,' or 'at' and does something at a later time rather than immediately. It triggers only once unless it has a stated duration (e.g., 'this turn'). Its source and controller are determined by how it was created: if created by a spell, the source is that spell and the controller is the spell's controller [603.7d]; if created by an activated or triggered ability, the source is the same as that ability's source and the controller is the player who controlled that ability as it resolved [603.7e]; if created by a static ability's replacement effect, the source is the object with that static ability and the controller is that object's controller at the time the replacement effect was applied [603.7f]; if created by a static ability that allows a player to take an action, the source is the object with that static ability and the controller is that object's controller at the time the action was taken [603.7g]. A delayed triggered ability that refers to a particular object still affects it even if its characteristics change, but not if the object has left its expected zone and returned as a new object [603.7c]. If the delayed triggered ability refers to X, and the creating spell or ability had a chosen value for X, the triggered ability uses that same value [107.3n]. Reflexive triggered abilities (e.g., 'When you do, ...') follow the same rules but are checked immediately after being created [603.12].",
   "citations": [
    "Delayed Triggered Ability",
    "603.7",
    "603.7a",
    "603.7b",
    "603.7c",
    "603.7d",
    "603.7e",
    "603.7f",
    "603.7g",
    "603.7h",
    "603.12",
    "610.2",
    "107.3n",
    "603.3a"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q031",
   "question": "can non-permanent spells have abilities?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2007,
    "completion_tokens": 302,
    "total_tokens": 2309,
    "cost": 0.000230382,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000230382,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000176022,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 5.436e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Yes, non-permanent spells (instant and sorcery spells) can have abilities. While on the stack, an instant or sorcery spell is an object with all the characteristics of the card associated with it, including any abilities printed on that card. These abilities are called spell abilities and function only while the spell is on the stack, as per rule [113.6]. For example, an instant spell with the text \"Counter target spell\" has a spell ability that functions on the stack. Additionally, some static abilities on instant or sorcery spells apply while on the stack, such as those that modify how the spell can be cast (rule [604.5]). However, activated and triggered abilities on the stack are not spells and are separate objects (rule [113.1c]), but the question asks about non-permanent spells themselves, which do have abilities as described.",
   "citations": [
    "113.6",
    "604.5",
    "113.1c",
    "405.4",
    "601.2a"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "q032",
   "question": "can I use a mana ability when a spell with split-second is on the stack?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
