  {
   "id": "c006",
   "question": "I cast a sorcery with buyback and pay its buyback cost, then copy that spell with [Fork]. Once everything resolves, how many copies of the card end up back in my hand?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2983,
    "completion_tokens": 353,
    "total_tokens": 3336,
    "cost": 0.000327402,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000327402,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000263862,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 6.354e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Zero. The original sorcery with buyback returns to your hand because you paid the buyback cost. The copy created by Fork is not a card—it has no card associated with it—so it cannot go to any hand. When the copy resolves, it is put into its owner’s graveyard as the final part of resolution, but since it is a copy of a spell (not a card), state-based action [704.5e] causes it to cease to exist if it would ever be in a zone other than the stack. In practice, the copy resolves and then ceases to exist; it never goes to your hand. Fork’s own ruling [Fork ruling #8] confirms that copying a spell for which buyback was paid gives you nothing back because the copy has no card to return. The original card goes to your hand; the copy does not.",
   "citations": [
    "702.27a",
    "707.10",
    "707.10b",
    "112.1a",
    "704.5e",
    "608.2n",
    "Fork ruling #8",
    "Fork ruling #10"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "c007",
   "question": "I have a face-down manifested card that's really [Lightning Bolt]. It dies and I imprint it on [Mimic Vat]. Later I activate Mimic Vat's copy ability -- do I get a token?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 2635,
    "completion_tokens": 381,
    "total_tokens": 3016,
    "cost": 0.000301122,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000301122,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000232542,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 6.858e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you do not get a token. When the manifested Lightning Bolt dies, you may exile it face up (since it dies as a permanent, not as a card in exile). However, Lightning Bolt is an instant card, not a permanent card. Mimic Vat's activated ability creates a token that is a copy of a card exiled with it. According to [Mimic Vat ruling #0], if the exiled card is a nonpermanent card (like an instant), you cannot create a token that is a copy of a nonpermanent card, so no token is created. This is consistent with [111.12], which states that if an effect instructs a player to create a token that is a copy of a nonexistent object (here, a nonpermanent card cannot be a token's basis), no token is created. The fact that the card was face down while on the battlefield does not change this outcome, because once it is exiled, it is a face-up instant card (see [406.3a] and [406.3b] for exile face-up rules).",
   "citations": [
    "Mimic Vat ruling #0",
    "111.12",
    "406.3a",
    "406.3b"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
  {
   "id": "c008",
   "question": "My [Fiend Hunter]'s enters-the-battlefield ability is on the stack, about to exile a creature. I copy that ability with [Lithoform Engine] and target a second creature. Both get exiled. When Fiend Hunter later leaves the battlefield, do both come back?",
   "served_model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
   "provider": "DeepInfra",
   "temperature_sent": 0.0,
   "seed_sent": 42,
   "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 3404,
    "completion_tokens": 342,
    "total_tokens": 3746,
    "cost": 0.000294192,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 1024,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000294192,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000232632,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 6.156e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "Yes, both exiled creatures return when Fiend Hunter leaves the battlefield. The original enters-the-battlefield ability and the copy are linked to Fiend Hunter's leaves-the-battlefield ability. When the leaves-the-battlefield ability refers to \"the exiled card,\" it refers to all cards exiled by both the original ability and the copy. Therefore, when Fiend Hunter leaves the battlefield, both creatures are returned to the battlefield under their owners' control. This is supported by the Lithoform Engine ruling that explicitly states if an ability is linked to a second ability, copies of that first ability are also linked, and if the second ability refers to \"the exiled card,\" it refers to all cards exiled by the first ability and the copy. The linked abilities rules (607.2a, 607.3) further confirm that the second ability refers to all cards exiled by the first ability, and when multiple cards are exiled, the ability performs its action on each exiled card.",
   "citations": [
    "Lithoform Engine ruling #4",
    "607.2a",
    "607.3"
   ],
   "error": null,
   "raw_text": null
  },
