    "cost": 0.000310032,
    "is_byok": false,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
     "cached_tokens": 64,
     "cache_write_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0,
     "video_tokens": 0
    },
    "cost_details": {
     "upstream_inference_cost": 0.000310032,
     "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.000211032,
     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 9.9e-05
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you cannot cast Tibalt, Cosmic Impostor via cascade from exiling Valki, God of Lies. Cascade checks the mana value of the card you exile, and for a double-faced card, the mana value is always that of its front face (rule [712.8c]). Valki’s front face has mana value 2. Cascade requires the exiled nonland card’s mana value to be *less than* the cascade spell’s mana value (rule [702.85a]). If your cascade spell’s mana value is, say, 5, then Valki’s mana value of 2 is less than 5, so it is a legal hit. However, cascade then allows you to cast that card *without paying its mana cost* only if the resulting spell’s mana value is less than the cascade spell’s mana value. When you cast a double-faced card, by default you cast it with its front face up (rule [712.11c]), so you would cast Valki (mana value 2), not Tibalt. There is no rule or effect here that lets you cast it “transformed” or “converted” (see [712.11d] and [702.162a] for such exceptions). Therefore, you cannot cast the Tibalt side via cascade. If you choose to cast Valki, its mana value (2) must be less than the cascade spell’s mana value; if it is, you may cast it for free. If you do not cast Valki, you put all exiled cards on the bottom of your library in a random order (rule [702.85a]).",
   "citations": [
    "712.8c",
    "702.85a",
    "712.11c",
    "712.11d",
    "702.162a"
   ],
