     "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 0.00012618
    },
    "completion_tokens_details": {
     "reasoning_tokens": 0,
     "image_tokens": 0,
     "audio_tokens": 0
    }
   },
   "answered": true,
   "text": "No, you do not get two tokens. The Lithoform Engine copies the activated ability of Mimic Vat, putting a second instance of that ability on the stack. When each ability resolves, it checks whether a card is exiled with Mimic Vat at that moment. If only one card was exiled (the imprinted creature), both abilities will see that same single card and each will create one token copy of it, resulting in two tokens total. However, if no card is exiled when either ability resolves (e.g., because the imprinted card was moved), that ability creates no token. The key point is that the copy of the ability is a separate instance on the stack, and each resolves independently, checking the current exiled cards. The rules for copying abilities ([707.10b]) state that a copy of an ability is put on the stack and is controlled by the player who put it there, and it has the same source as the original ability ([Lithoform Engine ruling #0]). The Mimic Vat ruling [#4] confirms that the token created is a copy of whatever card is exiled at the time the ability resolves. Since the copy ability is a separate instance, it does not share the same resolution as the original; each resolves on its own, and if a card is exiled at that time, each creates a token. Therefore, with one card exiled, you get two tokens (one from the original ability, one from the copy). If the exiled card were somehow removed before either resolves, you would get zero tokens from that ability. The rules do not prevent the copy from creating a token; the copy is a full independent ability on the stack. The only scenario where you would not get two tokens is if the exiled card is no longer there when one of the abilities resolves, but that is a matter of timing, not a rule against copying. Thus, the answer is yes, you get two tokens, assuming the imprinted card remains exiled through both resolutions.",
   "citations": [
